task: input-depth-both-wires
agent: opus-depth
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/input-depth-both-wires
worktree: ../okay-wt-input-depth
spec: specs/codecs.md ("Unknown fields, on both wires" — its two overstated claims)

why: the operator read the cbor-unknown-fields entry back and asked for
  it to be FIXED. Two of its claims are not true, and each hides
  something real.

  (1) "Every other read here recurses on the depth of the SCHEMA,
  which the program wrote" — the justification for Cbor.maxSkipDepth.
  It is false on the other wire. `JsonValue.Parser.value` and Json's
  CST projection (`into`) both recurse on the depth of the INPUT, with
  no bound, and MEASURED 2026-09-10 (java -cp probe, default stack):
  JsonValue.parse throws StackOverflowError at 20 000 nested arrays,
  the projection between 1 000 and 5 000 — where JsonValue.parse's own
  doc promises "never a throw" and Json.parse promises a value. So the
  CBOR skip is not the only input-depth recursion; it is the only
  BOUNDED one. (`JsonStrict.skipValue` is the counterexample that
  proves the shape: a bracket counter in a loop, no stack, no limit.)

  (2) "TestCompat.reads decodes on BOTH wires and requires them to
  agree, so the law is enforced for every case there" — the helper
  does, but removing the `Wire` parameter turned
  `assert(x(Wire.Json).compatible && x(Wire.Cbor).compatible)` into
  `assert(x.compatible && x.compatible)` in five places (the same
  conjunct twice, asserting half of what it reads as), and the
  "REMOVED case is the mirror" test never calls a decoder at all.

what: bound the JSON input-depth recursion at the same 256 the CBOR
  skip uses, in both JSON roads and in each road's own idiom — the
  fast parser is "not sure" (None), the lossless projection makes the
  cut a JErr, exactly as it does for every other damage — so neither
  road can throw; then say the true thing in Cbor.skipItem's comment,
  specs/codecs.md and docs/modules/okay-codec.md. And repair the Wire
  residue in TestCompat: collapse the doubled conjuncts into what they
  were meant to assert, and make every case that has a value run the
  decoders, so "enforced for every case" is a fact.

predict: the depth bound costs nothing measurable (one Int compared
  per nesting level on a road that allocates a Vector per level), and
  the remaining hole is a RECURSIVE schema's own decode — a Tree
  nested deep by the SENDER recurses on both wires with no bound.
  Measure it in this lane; if it dies, it is a BACKLOG entry, not this
  lane's fix (it needs depth threaded through every decoder).
